  [38;5;153mreason[39m, [38;5;153mstatus[39m, [38;5;153merrorCode[39m, [38;5;153mdurationMs[39m, [38;5;153mattempt[39m. [38;5;153mpassword[39m is not on it and cannot be added — I'll add a hard deny of
  [38;5;153mpassword[39m/[38;5;153mtoken[39m/[38;5;153msecret[39m that throws in tests if anyone allowlists them, so the belt-and-braces failure is a red test,
  not a leak.

  One more thing gets sanitized: the browser adapter logs the page URL, and URLs carry query strings. I'll log
  [38;5;153mlocation.pathname[39m only, never the query or fragment.

  [1mOne wrinkle on [38;5;153muserRef[39m worth your call[0m

  You chose a hashed identifier, and there's a catch I don't want to bury. A cryptographic hash needs
  [38;5;153mcrypto.subtle.digest[39m in the browser, which is [1masync[0m — that would make every log call async, which is a bad API. The
  sync options are a fast non-cryptographic hash (FNV-1a) with a deployment salt. That's fine against casual reading of
  the log store, but usernames are low-entropy: anyone holding both the logs and a user list could brute-force the
  mapping. A hash of an email is still personal data under GDPR, not an anonymization.

  The genuinely clean alternative is a [1mrandom per-session id[0m with no derivation from the username at all. Correlation
  then works the other way round: the error UI shows the user their session id, and they quote it in a support ticket.
  You lose "show me everything this user ever did", you gain a log store with no personal data in it.

  I lean toward the random session id, but it's a real tradeoff against support workflow and you may have a reason to
  want user-level correlation.
